1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Why the kernel is slow for packets — AxioByte Systems

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Start with the number nobody mentions. A 10-gigabit link, smallest 64-byte packets…

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
…is 14.88 million packets per second.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Divide that into a 3-gigahertz core and you get about 201 CPU cycles — roughly 67 nanoseconds — per packet.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
That's your entire time budget for one packet.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Now here's the problem: a single last-level cache miss costs around 95 nanoseconds.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
One cache miss and the whole budget is already gone.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
So watch where the kernel spends that budget on the normal path.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The NIC DMAs the packet into a kernel buffer — an sk_buff — a couple-hundred-byte allocation per packet.

10
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:43,000
Then an interrupt fires. The CPU saves and restores state; at 15 million a second, that becomes livelock.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,500
The TCP/IP stack runs, the sk_buff is freed, and your caches get trashed.

12
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,000
Then a context switch and a second copy, into user space, when you call recv.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
That's roughly 1800 cycles against a 200-cycle budget — about nine times over, on every packet.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Even Linux concedes: under load, NAPI switches the kernel to polling. Polling wins.

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,500
DPDK takes that idea all the way — and deletes each cost. Here's the map.

16
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,500
A poll-mode driver: no interrupts, no livelock.

17
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:14,000
Hugepages: far fewer TLB misses.

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
An mbuf mempool: buffers are pre-allocated with per-core caches — no per-packet allocation.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Zero-copy: the packet is a pointer, and you process it in place.

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,500
A burst of 32: per-packet overhead divided by thirty-two.

21
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:31,500
And prefetch: hide memory latency behind the work you're already doing.

22
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:35,500
Here's the receive loop for real — and notice there's no syscall.

23
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:40,500
rte_eth_rx_burst grabs up to 32 packets in a single poll of the NIC.

24
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:44,500
You prefetch the next packet while you work on the current one.

25
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:48,500
And you process in place — the mbuf is just a pointer. No copies.

26
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:53,500
The payoff: from about half a million packets a second to fifteen to thirty million, per core.

27
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:59,000
The price: a whole core pinned at 100% polling, and no kernel stack — you rebuild what you need.

28
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,500
So use it for routers, firewalls, load balancers, NFV — and skip it for a normal web app, where the kernel is fine.

29
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:09,500
Recap: you get one cache miss of budget per packet; the kernel spends nine times that; DPDK removes each cost.

30
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,000
Half a million becomes tens of millions per core.

31
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
